<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The only path this app shares, and it shares it with the installer alone.

  files-path is app-internal storage. Deliberately not external-path: an .apk on external storage is
  readable and writable by anything else on the phone, so it could be swapped between the download
  finishing and the installer opening it - and the owner would be confirming an install of something
  they never saw the version number of.

  Must stay in step with UpdateInstaller.downloadDir.
-->
<paths>
    <files-path name="updates" path="updates/" />
    <!--
      The phone's own certificate, offered to the laptop through the share sheet - REQ-0009 finding 1,
      which is what removed the adb-and-instrumentation route to pairing.

      files-path again, for the same reason: app-internal storage, so nothing else on the phone can
      swap the file between this app writing it and the receiving app reading it. The certificate is
      public, so the risk is not disclosure - it is the owner pinning a certificate that is not this
      phone's, which is why the screen shows the fingerprint to check.

      A SUBDIRECTORY, deliberately. The paired laptop's profile lives in files/pairing/ and carries the
      laptop's address; only files/pairing/outgoing/ is shareable, so that address is never inside the
      exposed tree at all.

      Must stay in step with CertificateHandover.DIRECTORY. The coupling is held by
      PhoneProvisionsItselfTest.theCertificateBecomesSomethingTheOwnerCanSend, which calls shareIntent
      for real - getUriForFile throws for a path this file does not cover.
    -->
    <files-path name="pairing" path="pairing/outgoing/" />
    <!--
      There was a `pairing_incoming` entry here, granting the system camera app write access to
      files/pairing/incoming/scan.jpg. REQ-0019 removed it with the route: the viewfinder reads frames
      in this process, so no other app is handed a place to write and nothing arrives from outside.

      One grant fewer. Removing an entry from this file is the only change here that reduces what
      another application may touch, which is worth a line rather than a silent deletion.

      LeftoverScans deletes the directory itself on start, because a phone that ran the old build may
      still be holding a photograph of the laptop's address.
    -->
</paths>
